WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.160
"The Dangerous Effects
of Heavy Metal Music"

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.640
If you search for heavy metal in the
National Library of Medicine database,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.480
most of what you find is on heavy metal
contamination in fish, for example,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.880
making it so hard to clearly establish
the role of fish in a healthy diet,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.940
perhaps helping to explain the
quintupling of odds

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.440
of autoimmune diseases
like juvenile arthritis for instance.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.080
But searching the hazards of
heavy metal also pops up entries

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.840
like this, on the “risks
from heavy metal music.”

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.080
Here they were talking about traumatic
injuries from slamming around,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.680
but you’re more likely to get
injured at an alternative rock concert.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.584
It’s the Goo Goo Dolls you’ve got
to worry about, not Nine Inch Nails.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.440
Okay sure, music-induced hearing
loss is a serious problem,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.240
but that can be from any loud music.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.431
It’s interesting, clinical
recommendations

00:01:00.431 --> 00:01:04.533
include the “80–90 rule”:
no more than 80%

00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:07.040
of the maximum volume
on personal listening devices

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.400
for no more than 90 minutes a day.
But that’s not what the science shows;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.338
“do not exceed 60 percent
of the maximum volume”

00:01:16.338 --> 00:01:18.260
may be more evidence-based,
but they figure

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.760
teens would just ignore that,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.207
so they came up with more
“acceptable” advice.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.600
I assumed I’d see a lot of satanic
panic nonsense from the 80’s,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.616
where parents bereaved by suicide
started suing heavy metal musicians.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.920
What kind of evidence
did the parents present?

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.360
There has been little scholarly research
until... this study that proceeded

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.200
to try to correlate the number
of statewide heavy metal magazine

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.115
subscriptions to youth suicide rates.

00:01:48.115 --> 00:01:51.409
Oh, you’ve got to be kidding.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.853
It got really crazy, though,
when researchers

00:01:54.853 --> 00:01:58.272
called psychiatric institutions
pretending to be parents

00:01:58.272 --> 00:02:01.848
worried because their son started
listening to that heavy metal music,

00:02:01.848 --> 00:02:04.842
even though they made it clear
that their son didn’t exhibit

00:02:04.842 --> 00:02:06.240
any symptoms of mental illness,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.960
no drugs, no alcohol,
and was doing fine at school.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.360
Ten of the twelve facilities believed

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.760
the son required
psychiatric hospitalization.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.400
Imagine what that would do to a kid!

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.960
In turns out if you actually
come back a few decades later,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.640
metalheads were signiﬁcantly
happier in their youth and appear

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.240
better psychologically
adjusted than their peers.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.320
Some studies were just strange.
Do Parkinson’s patients walk better

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.000
listening to “Yellow Submarine”
or “Master of Puppets”?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.070
Other studies were just like, duh.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.120
Heavy metal musicians exhibit
a higher heart rate than those

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.600
performing “contemporary Christian.”
Not much of a shocker.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.520
Some studies were kind of cute:
the Influence of music on promoting

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.857
patient safety during
surgery—veterinary patients.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.360
Kitties getting spayed with
little earphones on their head.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.077
It turns out that “Adagio for Strings”
may be more relaxing than AC/DC.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.280
A review on music therapy
for human patients warned that

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.400
caution should be exercised
when guiding patients in selecting

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.400
their music, as “chaotic music,”
such us hip-hop and metal,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.200
is not healing to human cells—
with three citations no less,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.000
though two of them don’t
say anything, and the third

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.680
is a nursing newsletter merely
quoting someone’s opinion.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.960
But I did some digging, and it turns
out stomach cancer cells like metal.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.280
If you play them "Cannibal Corpse"
versus Beethoven, 12 hours of

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.240
death metal increased their growth
in a petri dish (that’s so metal).

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.944
But who puts headphones on their stomach?

00:03:42.944 --> 00:03:45.590
Or their chests, for that matter?

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.640
While in one study,
Mozart killed off one type

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.480
of breast cancer cell
line and not another;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.600
in another study, only Beethoven’s
5th Symphony seemed to work,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.865
and Mozart flopped when the petri
dishes were surrounded

00:03:56.865 --> 00:03:59.188
by speakers on a little platform.

00:03:59.188 --> 00:04:01.805
How do they even get
this stuff published?

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.120
Anyway, the true danger from
heavy metal is headbanging.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.309
"Headbanging is a contemporary
dance form consisting of abrupt

00:04:10.309 --> 00:04:12.466
ﬂexion–extension
movements of the head,"

00:04:12.466 --> 00:04:15.040
commonly seen in heavy metal.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.320
The number of avid aﬁcionados
is unknown, but some fans

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.760
might be endangered
by indulging excessively.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.120
Although generally considered
harmless, health complications

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.680
attributed to this practice
include ripping your carotid artery,

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.960
rupturing your lung, whiplash
injury, and neck fracture,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.320
or in this case a subdural haematoma.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.120
This guy reported headbanging
at a Motörhead concert,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.400
and all that brisk forward and
backward acceleration might have

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.520
ruptured his bridging veins and
caused him to bleed into his skull.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.520
Bridging veins bridge the gap
between the brain and the covering

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.720
that lines the inside of
your skull, and if the veins tear,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.960
blood can build up under your
skull and compress your brain.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.120
This bridging vein rupture has been
demonstrated on headbanging cadavers

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.656
(again, a very metal study).

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.640
It’s been likened to a kind of
shaken-baby syndrome in adults.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.600
The researchers conclude
that their case serves as evidence

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.800
in support of Motörhead’s
rock-and-roll reputation,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.320
but I think the real takeaway is that
a potentially dangerous complication

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.000
like that can result from
a seemingly benign activity.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.720
And some of the brain
bleeds can be massive.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.569
Hmm, why did he have a headache
after headbanging at a party?

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.680
Here’s the CT scan. This is all
blood, squooshing his brain over.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.552
It’s amazing he survived,
though this poor guy didn’t.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.400
See, you can tear
more than just veins.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.120
There are two sets of arteries
that tunnel into the skull—

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.280
the carotid arteries in the front and
the vertebral arteries in the back—

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.000
and you can tear both sets.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.200
A 15-year-old indulged in
headbanging, ripping his carotid artery,

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.640
which led to a massive stroke;
and he presented half paralyzed,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.840
and unable to speak, and died
in a coma within a week.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.840
What about the vertebral
arteries in the back?

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.200
They’re wedged into your skull
rendering them susceptible to

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.000
shearing forces from
extremes of neck motion,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.960
and that’s exactly what
appeared to happen:

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.800
a heavy metal drummer tearing
the wall of the artery.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.320
Now obviously, all this is
really rare, probably afflicting

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.640
less than one in a thousand or so.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.440
What can metalheads
do to reduce their risk?

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.880
To prevent injury due to such
head-banging, the range of head

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.500
and neck motion should be reduced;

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.661
slower-tempo music
should replace metal

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.160
(good luck with that); the frequency
of head-banging could be only

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.960
on every second beat (that’s
actually not a bad idea);

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.994
or personal protective
equipment should be used

00:06:52.994 --> 00:06:55.625
(what like a neck brace?).

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.040
Little formal injury research has
been conducted on the world-wide

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.160
phenomenon of head banging;
so, researchers constructed

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.840
a theoretical head banging
model with enough physics terms

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.160
to make any nerd happy: angular
displacement, sinusoidal motion

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.680
in the sagittal plane, amplitude
of the displacement curve.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.160
Study participants: head bangers.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.344
But you do need a control group:
easy listening music.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.200
Head injury curves; neck injury curves,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.960
based on headbanging
tempo and angular sweep.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.760
At an average head banging
tempo, we’re trying to keep

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:36.240
the range of motion under 75 degrees,
so something like this.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.360
So, to minimize the risk of
head and neck injury, head bangers

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:42.216
should decrease their range
of head and neck motion.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.080
Oh, there’s that “personal
protective equipment” again.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.480
“Unfortunately, it is
difﬁcult, if not impossible,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.680
to change the habits of
heavy metal aﬁcionados.”

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.840
Maybe what we need are
metal-studded neck braces.

